# Worktree procedure

Read this before work that runs in a task worktree: every `spec` and `proposal` activity in `direct` mode under `[GIT_POLICY: worktree=required ...]` (`[tool.spec] worktree`), and any task the `GIT_POLICY` levels in [git policy](git-policy.md) put in a worktree under `auto`, from the first step to the cleanup after merge. A request that names the checkout ("on main", "in this worktree") overrides the policy for that task only; record the deviation as a user request, without its wording, in the final change report's assumptions.

Policy is read from the checkout a command runs in, so a running branch sees a base policy change only after merging the base. `devkit worktree` performs the deterministic steps; the session only changes directory. `<base>` is the checkout the request arrived in; `<branch>` is the activity branch, `plan-<slug>` or `dev-<slug>` ([branches](branches.md)). The worktree is created from the base HEAD; the base working tree is never touched. On a setup or sync warning from `start`, run its `[FIX]` before any work.

## 1. Start (in `<base>`, before any bundle or edit)

- `proposal` depth, Planning: `devkit worktree start plan-<slug>` and the printed `cd`; the proposal bundle is the unit.
{% if execution.environment == "local" %}- `proposal` depth, Development with native MCP: when `spec_handoff` is available, call it with the accepted proposal's `slug` from the retained base. It follows `proposal-handoff=ask|auto`: under `ask` its form shows the goal, accepted plan, branch/worktree and setup effects and collects the Development decision and effort once; under `auto` policy authorizes the same effects without a form, using the selected `dev_effort` hint (medium when absent). It creates the worktree and performs handoff after authority and readiness are checked, so do not create the branch first or ask the same approval in chat. Continue in the returned `worktree.path` with the returned effort as run input, run `next_command` and the post-handoff link check. Record the applied effort in the run's worker envelope. `needs_recovery` preserves the created worktree and reports setup/sync or handoff errors: inspect it and follow its advisories, never delete it or retry a fresh start automatically. This does not approve final integration. An unavailable native form uses the explicit CLI path below; it never implies approval.
{% else %}{% endif %}- `proposal` depth, Development through CLI: first establish `proposal-handoff` authority (existing exact user authorization under `ask`, or effective owning-base/task policy under `auto`). The low-level command executes the instructed handoff; invocation itself is no approval evidence. Once the accepted proposal is on the base (its Planning branch merged, or a direct acceptance committed there), run `devkit worktree start dev-<slug>` and the printed `cd` (or `--no-worktree`, which switches the base checkout), then hand off **on** `dev-<slug>` as Development's first step: `devkit spec proposal handoff <slug>` (refused on any other branch; run any `[FIX]` it prints; `SPEC_PROPOSAL_ACCEPTANCE_UNRECORDED` requires inspecting acceptance authority and eligibility), then check the proposal's premises against the current base. The base keeps the proposal until the merge.
- `session` depth bound for a PR: the `dev-<slug>` branch, in a worktree (`devkit worktree start dev-<slug>` and the printed `cd`) only when the `GIT_POLICY` levels call for one, else with `--no-worktree`; no bundle.
- `spec` depth (inline Planning): `devkit worktree start dev-<slug>`, the printed `cd`, then `devkit spec new <slug>` (`--workflow sdd-spec-bugfix` for a defect), which creates and binds `specs/<YYYY-MM>/<YYYYMMDD>-<slug>/` and registers scope in linked worktrees; record `plan-intent` and commit: that state is Development's input.
- A refused `start` changes nothing. For `WORKTREE_BRANCH_EXISTS` (the branch exists locally or on a remote, also with its worktree registered) continue it ([branches](branches.md) §3, which reuses the worktree) or choose another slug.

## 2. Work (in the worktree)

- Run the stages (`spec-specify`, `spec-plan`, `spec-tasks`, build, `spec-review`, `spec-sync`), checking each stage boundary with `devkit workflow next --unit <dir>`. `spec-tasks` and `spec-sync` run `devkit spec scope update --spec <dir>`; `devkit spec scope query` shows other tasks' overlaps, which inform scheduling and integration order, never block.
{% if execution.environment == "local" %}- Commit on `<branch>` in this worktree at every stage boundary (`git commit`; the commit gate runs there). Nothing is pushed to the remote or merged into the base before `dev-integration`; preparing and checking the branch against the local base may precede native approval.
{% else %}- Commit on `<branch>` in this worktree at every stage boundary (`git commit`; the commit gate runs there). Nothing is pushed to the remote or merged into the base before `dev-integration`; preparing and checking the branch against the local base may precede integration approval.
{% endif %}
{% if execution.environment == "local" %}## 3. Request native approval, integrate and finish (in `<base>`)
{% else %}## 3. Obtain integration authority, integrate and finish (in `<base>`)
{% endif %}
Authority for each path is the [completion policy](completion.md#policy).

{% if execution.environment == "local" %}- `devkit worktree finish` and `integration_integrate` run devkit-spec's post-finish cleanup in the base. Run a failed cleanup's `[FIX]` in the base once its cause is resolved (or `integration_resume` when `integration_integrate` stopped in `status: "partial"`). On `POST_FINISH_EXTENSION_FAILED` run both by hand: `devkit spec scope sweep --apply` and `devkit spec proposal prune <slug>`. A refused finish runs no cleanup, so the task stays registered; `devkit spec scope release -w <worktree path>` withdraws an abandoned task.
{% else %}- `devkit worktree finish` runs post-finish cleanup in the base only under separately existing CLI authority. Follow its refusal directives. On `POST_FINISH_EXTENSION_FAILED`, run `devkit spec scope sweep --apply` and `devkit spec proposal prune <slug>` only after resolving the cause. A refused finish leaves the task registered.
{% endif %}- A conflict in `.devkit/installation.json` when integrating the base (two tasks re-synced bundle assets): keep either side, run `devkit sync`, stage the receipt it writes.
- Local merge (`pull_request=auto`, no PR):
{% if execution.environment == "local" %}  - **MCP path (primary when `devkit` MCP tools are available)**: after branch preparation, verification and the final report, call `integration_integrate` with `branch`, `task_kind="unit"`, `task_id` and `unit` (session tasks: `task_kind="session"`, `task_id` and `claims`). It verifies the exact scope, derives policy authority from shared base `dev-integration=auto` without confirmation (ask confirms natively), lands the task as a merge commit built outside the base checkout (a fast-forward under `[worktree] merge = "ff-only"`; no fetch, push or PR), absorbing target commits that land during the confirmation when the merge is clean, and finishes the worktree. `integration_prepare` is an optional read-only preflight; inspect a run with `integration_status(request_id, detail=true)` and resume a `partial` one with `integration_resume`.
  - **CLI fallback / manual recovery** (`INTEGRATION_CONFIRMATION_UNAVAILABLE`, or invoked directly after `dev-integration` approval): `devkit worktree finish <branch> --merge --local-only`, or without `--local-only` to also fetch the base's upstream and push the base (never forced). It refuses a branch whose units hold an unresolved problem or unanswered question (`WORKTREE_BLOCKED`, [worker records](worker-records.md)). Follow the `push=` field of `[WORKTREE_FINISHED: ...]` and any `[FIX]`; on `push=no-upstream` report that nothing was pushed. Then run `devkit rules check --ci --base <merge-base>` on the result and assert the runner checks yourself, since no CI runs without a PR: `devkit workflow next --unit <dir> --given dev-integration --given lint-ci-clean --given merged`.
{% else %}  - **Explicit CLI route** (only with separately existing `dev-integration` approval): `devkit worktree finish <branch> --merge --local-only`, or without `--local-only` to also fetch the base's upstream and push the base (never forced). It refuses a branch whose units hold an unresolved problem or unanswered question (`WORKTREE_BLOCKED`, [worker records](worker-records.md)). Follow the `push=` field of `[WORKTREE_FINISHED: ...]` and any `[FIX]`; on `push=no-upstream` report that nothing was pushed. Then run `devkit rules check --ci --base <merge-base>` on the result and assert the runner checks yourself, since no CI runs without a PR: `devkit workflow next --unit <dir> --given dev-integration --given lint-ci-clean --given merged`.
{% endif %}- Through a PR (`pull_request=required`, or chosen under `auto`): push `<branch>`, open the PR and let it merge only while `devkit workflow envelope check --unit <dir> --format json` on its head reports `"blocked": false` and no `pending_questions`; after the merge run `devkit worktree finish <branch>` (no `--merge`). The merge test is an ancestor test, so a PR merged by squash or rebase stays `WORKTREE_UNMERGED`: remove that worktree and branch only with the user's authorization.
{% if execution.environment == "local" %}- A refusal names its cause and `[FIX]` and leaves earlier steps done; the same command resumes once it is resolved. `WORKTREE_MERGE_NOT_FF`, `WORKTREE_MERGE_CONFLICT` or `INTEGRATION_RECEIPT_CONFLICT`: merge the base into the branch (`spec-integrate` Step 1; never rebase), re-verify and integrate again. `INTEGRATION_TARGET_MOVED` changed nothing: resume while the target still equals the last build target, else integrate again. Never remove a dirty worktree by force (`WORKTREE_DIRTY`: commit or discard in the worktree). Unlock a `WORKTREE_LOCKED` worktree only once the user agrees. `WORKTREE_NOT_FOUND` for a merged branch devkit did not create: delete it (`git branch -d <branch>`) only with the user's agreement.
{% else %}- A refusal names its cause and `[FIX]` and leaves earlier steps done; the same command resumes once it is resolved. `WORKTREE_MERGE_NOT_FF`, `WORKTREE_MERGE_CONFLICT` or `INTEGRATION_RECEIPT_CONFLICT`: merge the base into the branch (`spec-integrate` Step 1; never rebase), re-verify and integrate again. Never remove a dirty worktree by force (`WORKTREE_DIRTY`: commit or discard in the worktree). Unlock a `WORKTREE_LOCKED` worktree only once the user agrees. `WORKTREE_NOT_FOUND` for a merged branch devkit did not create: delete it (`git branch -d <branch>`) only with the user's agreement.
{% endif %}
